{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3181","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3181","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3181\",\"parent_revision\":\"9973a0a3452ec889670f5e50ecfe3872489f1ce050a5ba92403efa9bf76506d9\",\"root_revision\":\"9973a0a3452ec889670f5e50ecfe3872489f1ce050a5ba92403efa9bf76506d9\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Docs\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`.claude/CLAUDE.md` contains two routing protocols that can both apply to the same task, and never says how they compose.\\n\\nThe Task Classification flowchart routes a task classified as \\\"Fix\\\" (known bug, CI failure, broken behavior) to `.claude/rules/fix-delegation-discipline.md`, whose cycle is reproduce first, then fix only what the root cause statement names, then validate against the same reproduction command.\\n\\nThe Agent Delegation Standards section routes Python implementation work to `@python-engineering:adversarial-solution-design` before implementing, then `@python-engineering:python-cli-architect` taking that output as its brief.\\n\\nA Python bug fix satisfies both. Nothing states whether the adversarial pass runs before the reproduction, after it, or instead of it \u2014 nor whether the reproduction-first cycle is something the architect agent inherits, something the orchestrator performs before delegating, or something the adversarial agent's validation plan is expected to supply.\\n\\nWhy this matters rather than being a wording nit: `.claude/rules/prose-file-classification.md` classifies CLAUDE.md as an LLM prompt-engineering artifact, where ambiguity is a defect rather than a style issue, and states that an agent reading two conflicting rules will choose one arbitrarily. Both paths are individually sound, so whichever an agent picks it can justify \u2014 which means the choice will vary run to run with no signal that anything was skipped. The specific risk is an agent taking the delegation path and never writing a reproduction, since the adversarial agent produces a behavioral validation plan that superficially resembles one but is not the same artifact as a failing command captured before any code change.\\n\\nObserved context: this gap was surfaced by a content review of a change to the Agent Delegation Standards section, and deliberately left unfixed there because resolving it means deciding how two protocols relate, not editing one block. The same session produced concrete evidence that the reproduction-first discipline is load-bearing \u2014 several defects were only distinguishable as silent wrong-writes rather than silent no-ops because the old code's behaviour was captured before the fix.\\n\\nUser-provided context: no fix direction is asserted here. Whether one protocol subsumes the other, whether they are sequential, or whether Task Classification should route Python fixes differently, is the design question to answer during grooming.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Found by content review of the Agent Delegation Standards edit, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"383bf3646a8d263e326048beddcf6af6c426c0a84e564a09b1fce63605b55184\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQPWmfA\"}"}